      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{6}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.84)^{6}.$ Give your answer to five decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}n(n-1)(n-2){x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{6} & =1+6x+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{6} \\\\  & =1+6x+15{x}^{2}+20{x}^{3}+⋯\\,+{x}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{6}≈1+6x+15{x}^{2}+20{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.16$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{6}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0.84)^{6} & =(1+(-0.16))^{6} \\\\  & ≈1+6(-0.16)+15(-0.16)^{2}+20(-0.16)^{3} \\\\  & =1-6(0.16)+15(0.025\\,6)-20(0.004\\,096) \\\\  & =1-0.96+0.384-0.081\\,92 \\\\  & =0.342\\,08,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places.",
